\paragraph{Safety as collision avoidance.} We formalize safety through the signed distance field between the robot's end-effector and scene obstacles. For a robot configuration $q$ in a scene with obstacle set $\mathcal{O}$, the signed distance $s(q) = \min_{o \in \mathcal{O}} \mathrm{SDF}(q, o)$ is positive when the end-effector is separated from all obstacles and negative when penetration occurs. A trajectory is considered safe if $s(q_t) \geq \delta$ for all timesteps, where $\delta > 0$ is a safety margin. Our goal is to train a policy that intrinsically generates trajectories satisfying this constraint, without requiring runtime collision checking or trajectory filtering.

\paragraph{Core insight.} Rather than imposing safety through constrained optimization at inference (which adds latency and may degrade task performance), we shape the flow-matching training objective so that the learned velocity field steers action trajectories away from collision regions. The SDF auxiliary head acts as a geometric probe during training: it reads the model's internal representation and provides a differentiable safety signal. When gradients from the collision-shaping loss propagate back through this probe into the action generation backbone, the model learns to associate its internal representations with collision-free trajectories.

