Several directions remain open. The current approach relies on SDF labels precomputed from simulator geometry; extending \ours{} to real-world settings will require neural SDF estimation from depth observations or point clouds. Evaluating \ours{} on additional VLA backbones beyond $\pi_{0.5}$, such as Octo and RT-2, would establish the generality of the internalization principle. Finally, real-world deployment with truly novel object categories and deformable or transparent obstacles remains an important validation step.

\subsection*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Single benchmark.} All experiments were conducted on LIBERO and SafeLIBERO in the MuJoCo simulator. Validating \ours{} on additional benchmarks such as ManiSkill, RoboCasa, or RLBench would strengthen confidence in its generality.

\paragraph{Single VLA backbone.} We evaluated \ours{} exclusively on $\pi_{0.5}$. Whether the collision-shaping signal transfers effectively to alternative architectures (diffusion-based policies like Octo, autoregressive action generation in RT-2) is an empirical question that this work does not address.

\paragraph{Known geometry assumption.} Ground-truth SDF labels were computed from MuJoCo's analytical geometry engine. In real-world deployment, scene geometry must be estimated from sensory input, and the impact of label noise on the collision-shaping loss has not been characterized.

\paragraph{Collision threshold sensitivity.} The safety margin $\delta = 2$\,cm was held fixed across all tasks. An adaptive or task-conditioned threshold could address scenarios with varying proximity requirements.

\paragraph{Simulation only.} All results are from simulated environments. A real-robot validation is needed to confirm that the internalized collision awareness survives the sim-to-real gap.

\subsection*{Broader Impact}

Safe robot manipulation is a prerequisite for deploying autonomous robots in human-occupied environments. \ours{} contributes by embedding collision awareness into VLA weights at training time, removing inference-time computational overhead. We note that the zero-overhead framing may create a false sense of completeness: training-time safety internalization complements rather than replaces runtime safety monitoring, particularly in high-stakes applications.


