The design of \ours{} draws a structural analogy to guidance distillation in image generation \citep{meng2023distillation}, where classifier-free guidance is internalized into model weights during training so that inference proceeds without guidance computation. In our setting, the SDF auxiliary head plays the role of the guidance signal: it shapes the velocity field during training, then is discarded at inference. This ``train-with, infer-without'' paradigm is not specific to collision avoidance. The same framework could, in principle, internalize other auxiliary objectives (task progress metrics, energy efficiency constraints, human comfort zones) by substituting the SDF head with a corresponding prediction target and shaping loss. The key requirement is that the auxiliary signal be expressible as a per-timestep scalar or low-dimensional quantity computable from the model's internal representations.

Our current implementation assumes access to analytical scene geometry for SDF label computation, which is readily available in simulation (MuJoCo mesh models) but not in unstructured real-world environments. Deformable objects, liquids, and transparent surfaces lack well-defined signed distance fields under the classical formulation. Learned SDF representations, such as DeepSDF \citep{park2019deepsdf} or neural implicit fields estimated from depth sensors or point clouds, offer a plausible extension path, though the effect of label noise from such estimators on the collision-shaping loss remains an open question. A related limitation concerns the scope of the safety guarantee. The collision-shaping loss operates on per-timestep signed distance values and does not directly optimize trajectory-level safety constraints. Constrained RL methods such as SCPO \citep{zhao2024scpo} and SafeVLA \citep{zhang2025safevla} can optimize cumulative safety cost over a full episode and thus provide stronger multi-step collision avoidance guarantees. However, these methods lack explicit geometric grounding and may struggle with precise spatial reasoning near obstacle boundaries. A hybrid approach that combines \ours{}'s per-timestep geometric shaping with trajectory-level constrained optimization could address both limitations.

From a scalability perspective, the SDF auxiliary head adds only ${\sim}$263K parameters to the 7B+ base model, a negligible overhead ($<$0.004\%). The SDF labels are scalar quantities computed offline from simulator geometry; no dense 3D field storage or online computation is needed during training. These properties suggest that the approach scales naturally to larger VLA backbones (such as the full $\pi_0$ at 3B+ parameters) and to environments with more complex obstacle configurations, since the per-timestep label cost remains constant regardless of model or environment scale.


